+# Acoustic scene / room-type classification from ambient audio
+Research note, 2026-09-28. Scope: the classification half only — given ambient sound (no emitted probe, ever), which environment is this? Hardware assumed: Pixel 9 Pro XL + Pixel Buds Pro 2 (LE Audio, mono 48 kHz, bud DSP in the path) and the phone's own 2-mic UNPROCESSED array.
+All DCASE numbers below were pulled from the official results tables on dcase.community (evaluation-dataset accuracy unless stated).
+
+## TL;DR
+- Compute is not the problem. DCASE's low-complexity limit is 128 kB of parameters and 30 MMACs per 1-s inference, and models inside it exist. At a 0.1–1 Hz decision cadence that is ~30 MFLOP/s — negligible next to the rendering load already measured in this project.
+- The sensor is the problem. The bud chain (NS/AGC/AEC) is optimised to remove the stationary noise and flatten the level, which are two of the main room cues. The phone's UNPROCESSED array is the only genuinely pre-DSP sensor and the only one with low frequencies and two channels.
+- Reverb buys you indoor/outdoor and a rough size prior, not room identity. Blind T60/DRR estimation from ambient noise is a solved-ish problem only with 4+ s of decaying signal, and RT60 ranges of bedroom/living room/classroom/carriage overlap heavily.
+- Realistic ceiling for the 5-class task with 5–10 s windows and in-domain training: ~75–88%, with bedroom↔living room the hard pair; outdoor and train carriage are easy. Silently, the honest answer is "unknown".
+
+## 1. Published benchmark numbers
+| Benchmark | Classes / clips | Baseline | Best | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| DCASE 2016 Task 1 (TUT ASC 2016) | 15 classes incl. home, office, classroom, library; 30-s | 77.2% (dev 72.5%) | 89.7% (CP-JKU, binaural+MFCC+spectrogram fusion) | closest published analogue of "room type" |
+| DCASE 2017 Task 1 (TUT 2017) | 15 classes; 10-s | 65.7% (CI 63.4–68.0) | 80.4% (Han/COCAI) | clip length alone costs ~9 points vs 2016 |
+| DCASE 2020 Task 1A (TAU Urban 2020 Mobile) | 10 classes, 1-s, 11 devices (5 unseen) | 51.4% | 76.5% (ETRI); several 75–76% | device+city shift is the killer |
+| DCASE 2020 Task 1B | 3 coarse classes (indoor/outdoor/transport), 1 device, ≤500 kB | 89.5% | 96.5% (CP-JKU) | the coarse split you actually need is easy |
+| DCASE 2021 Task 1A | 10 classes, 1-s | 45.6% | 76.1% (QTI ResNorm / BC-ResNet-Mod) | |
+| DCASE 2021 Task 1B | single-device low-complexity | 77.1% | 88.4% (AIT) | |
+| DCASE 2022 Task 1 | 10 classes, ≤128 kB int8, ≤30 MMAC/inference | 44.2% | 59.6% (CP-JKU) | same budget you'd use on a phone |
+| DCASE 2023 Task 1 | same limits | 44.8% | 62.7% (CP-JKU) | |
+| RevRIR 2024 (arXiv 2406.03120) | identify 1 of 110 rooms from reverberant speech | — | 40% | shows room-identity-from-reverb is hard |
+
+Read: coarse indoor/outdoor/transport is 89–96%; ten urban scenes at 1 s is 60–76%; 15 room-ish classes with 30-s clips is 77–90%. Longer windows and coarser classes both help more than architecture.
+
+## 2. Which features matter
+- **Log-mel** (e.g. 64 mels, 25 ms/10 ms) is what every competitive DCASE system uses; it is the default and there is no evidence favouring a replacement at the top. Cost is ~1.6 M multiply-adds per second of audio.
+- **MFCC + deltas** was the DCASE 2016/2017 baseline and sits ~12–15 points behind the winning CNNs. Still perfectly usable for a sub-200 kB model; deltas/context are cheap (+1–3 points typical).
+- **GTCC / gammatone cepstra** buy better low-frequency resolution and are the front end of the standard blind-T60 CNN (Gamper & Tashev, IWAENC 2018, ERB filterbank). Useful if you care mainly about the decay structure.
+- **Modulation spectrum (2–20 Hz envelope)** is the most reverb-specific cheap feature: a room acts as a low-pass on the temporal envelope, so the 2–20 Hz modulation energy drops with reverberation (Gergen & Martin, Interspeech 2015). This is the single most defensible hand-crafted feature for your task.
+- **Hand-crafted vs CNN**: hand-crafted is not competitive at the top of any DCASE edition (~10–15 points behind), but the 2016 winner was itself a fusion of MFCC + spectrogram + binaural features, and the 2021–2023 low-complexity winners are small log-mel CNNs. For a <200 kB model, log-mel + a small CNN is still the right choice.
+
+## 3. Models small enough for the phone
+- **YAMNet**: MobileNetV1 depthwise; 3.73 M parameters, 14.2 MB float; 0.15–0.32 ms per inference on Snapdragon 8 Gen 1/3 NPUs (Qualcomm AI Hub model card). Outputs 521 AudioSet event classes, i.e. events not room types, and it is 16 kHz mono so it discards everything above 8 kHz. Good prototyping shortcut, poor fit for a reverb cue and for the bud's ~10–13 kHz band.
+- **BC-ResNet / BC-ResNet-Mod** (Kim et al., Interspeech 2021; QTI DCASE 2021 Task 1A submission): broadcasted residual 1D+2D convolutions, designed for tiny budgets; the DCASE 2021 Task 1A winner ran at 76.1% with residual normalisation.
+- **The DCASE low-complexity envelope** is the honest on-device budget: ≤128 kB parameters, ≤30 MMACs per 1-s inference, and that bought 59.6% (2022) / 62.7% (2023) on 10-class 1-s. A 5-class task with 5–10 s aggregation sits well inside this.
+- **Blind-T60 CNN** (Adobe, arXiv 1909.03642): 8,737 trainable parameters, real-time factor 0.0088 (~110× real-time, CPU-only, 2018 MacBook Pro). A CRNN joint estimator (WASPAA 2021, arXiv 2010.11167) used 369 k params.
+- **Spatial features are cheap and pay off**: with 2 channels, inter-channel coherence / spectro-spatial covariance features halved T60/DRR/C50 estimation error versus single-channel spectral features (arXiv 2411.03172, 2024). This is an argument for the phone array rather than the mono bud stream.
+- **Budget summary**: log-mel front end ≈ 1.6 M mult/s; 100–200 k-param int8 CNN ≈ 100–200 kB and ≤30 MMACs per decision; a 20–30-dim reverb block ≈ 10^5 ops/s. Entirely inside a few tenths of a percent of one phone core. The measured reference in this repo is that the 7.1 binaural bed costs ~1032 µs per 10 ms block on an A55 — the classifier is two-to-three orders smaller than that.
+
+## 4. Does reverb actually distinguish room TYPE?
+Partly, and less than the goal statement hopes.
+- **Outdoor vs indoor is the strong reverb split**: outdoors there is essentially no late tail (DRR ≈ 0 dB), indoors it is −5 to −15 dB. This one is reliable and worth building on.
+- **RT60 separates room types weakly**: bedroom ~0.3–0.5 s, living room ~0.4–0.6 s, classroom ~0.5–0.9 s, train carriage ~0.3–0.8 s (carriage is small but highly reflective, then partly damped by seating). Absorption dominates over volume, so RT60 alone will not tell bedroom from living room.
+- **Blind T60/DRR from ambient sound without any excitation is exactly the ACE Challenge task** (Eaton et al., IEEE/ACM TASLP 2016; ACE results report arXiv 1606.03365). The report's own error breakdowns show accuracy depends on SNR (18 / 12 / −1 dB), on noise type (ambient, babble, fan) and on utterance length (<5 s, <15 s, ≥15 s). Practical rule from the Adobe work: discard/repeat anything under ~4 s, estimate every 0.5 s and take the median over stationary audio.
+- **What scene CNNs actually key on is the noise/event bed, not the impulse response.** The evidence is the benchmark structure: the same acoustic material is 89–96% when the decision is indoor/outdoor/transport and 60–76% when it is ten specific scenes, and performance drops ~10–15 points on unseen devices/cities. The discriminative signal is the background texture (train rumble, chatter, traffic, keyboard), with reverb as a helpful but secondary channel.
+
+## 5. Realistic ceiling and audio per decision
+- Standard ASC uses 1-s segments with 50% overlap and aggregates over ~10 s. At 1 s the DCASE 10-class multi-device systems are 60–76%; with a 5–10 s window and a 5-class, easier label set you should expect higher.
+- Working estimate for {bedroom, living room, classroom, train carriage, outside} with in-domain training and 5–10 s windows: **75–88% overall**, with outside and train carriage >90%, and bedroom vs living room ~60–70% pairwise. That is an extrapolation from the 2020 Task 1B (3-class, 89–96%) and TUT 2016 (15-class 30-s, 77–90%) numbers, not a measured result for this exact label set.
+- A 1-s decision is too noisy for preset switching; use 1 Hz posteriors over a 5–10 s rolling window, and require two consecutive windows to agree before changing preset.
+
+## 6. When it is quiet or silent
+- **Genuinely silent**: MEMS mic self-noise (~30 dB SPL-A equivalent) is uncorrelated, so a blind decay estimator sees a *dry* signal and will report something like "outdoors". Never update on that. Gate on input level and on non-stationarity (envelope kurtosis / spectral flux), freeze the posterior, decay confidence, and expose an explicit `unknown`. A 2.5–5 s minimum before the first decision is realistic.
+- **Quiet but not silent** (talking, TV, keyboard, footsteps): this is the regime the ACE estimators are designed for, and it works — non-stationary events give you decay slopes and modulation-spectrum cues. Purely stationary HVAC/fan noise is the bad case (ACE shows error growth toward −1 dB SNR).
+- Android caveat already established in this repo: silenced capture (or bud-DSP gating) must be treated as "no data", not as "quiet room".
+
+## 7. Earbud DSP vs phone array
+- The buds' own NS/AGC/AEC runs before LC3 and cannot be bypassed. NS attenuates stationary noise (train rumble, HVAC, fan — the carriage and classroom cues), AGC compresses level dynamics, AEC removes the user's own voice tail — all three are room cues. This repo's own recon recorded a loud continuous sweep arriving as near-digital-silence over HFP: the bud DSP will gate steady-state input.
+- If the usable bud band really is only ~10–13 kHz, the low-frequency rumble that separates a carriage from a classroom is gone as well.
+- Verdict: **the bud stream can support an event/activity detector ("someone is talking", "music is playing"), not a reliable reverb estimator.** The phone's UNPROCESSED 2-channel array at 48 kHz is the sensor for the reverb leg, and its inter-channel coherence is a genuinely useful extra feature.
+
+## 8. What I would build first, and why
+1. **Phone-array UNPROCESSED → 16 kHz, 64-mel log-mel + deltas, 100–200 k-param int8 DS-CNN/BC-ResNet-Mod**, trained for 5 classes with device/city augmentation. TFLite + NNAPI, 1 Hz inference, 5–10 s aggregation, hysteresis, level gate, `unknown` state.
+2. **Add a 20–30-dim hand-crafted reverb block** and late-fuse it: per-band envelope decay slopes (RT60 proxy) on detected decays, inter-channel coherence, 2–20 Hz modulation-spectrum energy, spectral kurtosis/flatness, level dynamics. This is what makes "outdoors", "carriage" and "classroom" separable when the event bed is ambiguous, and it costs nothing.
+3. **Train on TUT ASC 2016** (home/office/classroom/library) + **TAU Urban Acoustic Scenes 2020 Mobile** (train/tram/street/park) + your own labelled recordings, with the same device-simulation chain used at inference.
+4. **Earbuds only as a secondary/fallback event detector**, never as the reverb sensor.
+
+Why this order: the phone array is the only pre-DSP sensor, it has the low band and two channels, and the total classifier cost (<0.5 MB, a few MMACs/s) is dwarfed by the existing rendering budget. The bottleneck is labelled in-domain data, not compute.
+
