@@ -0,0 +1,228 @@
+# Blind RT60 / room identification from ambient audio — practical report
+
+Date: 2026-09-28. Scope: Stage 6 environment detection for virtual-room preset selection
+(ADR-0029, roadmap Stage 6). Constraints: totally silent (ambient sound only), on-device on
+Android, phone UNPROCESSED mic array vs Pixel Buds Pro 2 LE-Audio LC3 mic.
+
+## 0. Bottom line
+
+Build a **phone-array, 250 Hz–4 kHz octave-band DSP estimator** (energy-decay-curve + guarded
+free-decay fitting, per-band "no estimate"), plus a **modulation/envelope feature** for steady
+noise, and feed a small classifier. **Do not build the base design on the earbud mic** and
+**do not build the room classifier on RT60 alone** — measured per-class RT60 overlap (below)
+is the same size as the blind estimator's error.
+
+## 1. Two constraints that decide the design
+
+1. **The buds' mic is room ⊗ bud DSP.** Their NS/AGC/AEC runs before the codec; AGC is a
+   time-varying gain that destroys decay slopes and NS gates the tail. This repo already
+   established this (docs/research/pixel-buds-pro-2-mic-2026-09-22.md). Android's
+   UNPROCESSED source is the opposite: CDD 5.11 requires no AGC/NS/AEC and >=60 dB SNR per
+   mic. That is the only capture path that can carry a decay.
+2. **Silent means the source is unknown, so everything is blind.** RT60 is only observable
+   when the ambient field actually contains a decay that ends above the noise floor: speech
+   pauses, footsteps, door/keyboard transients, TV. In a genuinely silent room there is **no
+   excitation and no information** — no method in this report can return an RT60. The honest
+   output is "unknown" and the held/neutral preset, with non-acoustic context (accelerometer,
+   barometer, screen, ANC state) doing the work. Room tone at ~30–45 dB SPL is enough for the
+   modulation features; the decay-fitting methods need transients on top of it.
+
+## 2. Techniques
+
+Accuracy numbers below are from published evaluation sets, mostly **simulated reverberant
+speech**, so treat them as lower bounds on real-world error.
+
+### 2.1 Schroeder backward integration (the definition, not an estimator)
+Computes the energy decay curve (EDC) by integrating the squared impulse response backwards;
+T20/T30 from a line fit and extrapolation to 60 dB. Needs the IR (or a decay-only signal), and
+a fit range of −5…−25 dB (T20) or −5…−35 dB (T30) per ISO 3382-2, so a trustworthy T30 wants
+~45 dB of usable dynamic range. Cost: negligible. Role here: it is the ground-truth definition
+and the correct kernel once a decay segment has been *detected*; it is not itself blind.
+
+### 2.2 Free-decay detection + EDC line fit (Spectral Decay Distribution family)
+Detects candidate decays from log-energy drops, fits a slope, aggregates per band. This is what
+ACE's best single-channel fullband T60 systems did — the 2015 ACE Challenge found the best T60
+estimators were signal-processing approaches, not ML (Bryan 2019, section 1). Published error on
+the ACE evaluation set (reported in Bryan 2019, Table 2): QA-Reverb MSE 0.0648 → **RMSE ≈ 0.255 s**.
+Cost: negligible; a streaming envelope + line fits.
+
+### 2.3 Maximum likelihood over decay-rate statistics
+Ratnam et al. (JASA 2003) and Löllmann & Vary (arXiv:1511.04063, 2015). Model late reverberation
+as a decaying Gaussian process, estimate the decay rate rho by ML over pre-selected frames,
+T60 = 6.908/rho; sub-band estimates are combined energy-weighted, or low bands are extrapolated
+from upper bands. Robust because it estimates a distribution parameter rather than one slope.
+Frames of M = 16 000 samples at 16 kHz (1 s) were found accurate. Cost: low (filterbank +
+per-frame statistics). This is the strongest *classical* blind method.
+
+### 2.4 Spectral decay curve / spectral decay distribution (SDD)
+Histogram of per-frame decay rates in each band; the mode is insensitive to individual bad
+frames. Used in ACE entries (SDD/SDDSA). Cost: low.
+
+### 2.5 Cepstral / spectral-envelope features
+Cepstral peak prominence and similar mainly track STI/clarity, not RT60; they are weak and
+source-dependent. Not recommended for RT60.
+
+### 2.6 Modulation / envelope-based (MTF, SRMR, envelope modulation spectrum)
+Estimates how the source's amplitude modulation (2–8 Hz) is smeared, which is a function of
+reverberation. Works on **continuous** sound, so it is the only family that still works when
+the room is quiet-but-steady with no clean decays. Non-intrusive (blind) STI estimation from
+reverberant speech exists. Cost: low (envelope + modulation filterbank). Use as the fallback
+feature, not as the primary RT60.
+
+### 2.7 Neural / ML (2020+), single channel, no known excitation
+| System | Input | Published accuracy |
+|---|---|---|
+| Gamper & Tashev CNN (IWAENC 2018), re-scored on ACE | 4 s speech | MSE 0.0384 → **RMSE ≈ 0.196 s**, bias +0.030 s, rho 0.836 |
+| Bryan, AIR augmentation + CNN (arXiv:1909.03642, 2019) | 4 s speech | MSE 0.0261 → **RMSE ≈ 0.162 s**, bias −0.026 s, rho 0.920 |
+| CNN-encoder (Interspeech 2020 / TU Delft) | RIR / speech | MSE 0.0006 s² on clean RIRs (**RMSE 0.024 s**), 0.0011 s² at +30 dB SNR (0.033 s), **0.0282 s² on real recordings (RMSE 0.168 s)** |
+| Universal CRNN (Peso Parada et al., arXiv:2109.14436, 2021) | 8 s speech, SNR −5…24 dB, 369 K params | T60 **MAE 0.21 s**; DRR MAE 2.91 dB; C50 MAE 5.95 dB |
+| Götz et al., dynamic conditions (arXiv:2202.11790, 2022) | 4 s speech | static test MSE 0.0219 → RMSE 0.148 s, MAPE 18.9 % |
+| Ick et al., phase features (arXiv:2303.07449, 2023) | noisy speech | RT60 rho 0.946 (joint); normalized MSE 0.1408 |
+| Contrastive representation (arXiv:2302.11205, 2023) | speech | RT60 **RMSE 0.223 s** |
+| SS-BRPE, self-supervised attention (arXiv:2409.05212, 2024) | noisy speech, RT60 0.41–19.68 s | linear **MAE 0.39 s**, median abs 0.08 s, rho 0.972 |
+
+Reading: the modern nets are in the **0.13–0.25 s RMSE** band on speech corpora, and ~0.17 s on
+the one real-recorded test above. They beat the classical methods by ~0.05–0.1 s RMSE, not by an
+order of magnitude.
+
+## 3. Failure mode of naive free-decay fitting, and the guards
+
+**Failure mode.** The EDC of a real signal is not a straight line: it is a decay that bends into
+the noise floor. Fit a line over the whole thing and the floor drags the slope toward zero —
+because T60 = 6.908/rho, a slope that is only 0.5x too small returns an RT60 that is **2x too
+long**. In a quiet room with no real decay, you are fitting noise-floor fluctuations, rho ≈ 0, and
+the estimate diverges (bounded only by your clamp); a naive implementation on a 1 s window 20 dB
+above the floor readily returns 3–10 s for a 0.4 s room.
+
+**Guards that make it trustworthy** (1–4 are published practice, 5–9 are the thresholds I would set):
+
+1. **Fit range, not full range.** Fit only where the EDC is between −5 dB and
+   max(−35 dB, noise-floor + 5 dB). ISO 3382-2 evaluation ranges: T20 −5…−25 dB, T30 −5…−35 dB.
+2. **Noise-floor truncation.** Estimate the floor from the tail and truncate the fit iteratively
+   (Lundeby et al. method). Without this the slope is biased low, and the bias grows as SNR drops.
+3. **Minimum decay depth.** Require >= 15 dB (T20-equivalent) or >= 25 dB (T30-equivalent) of
+   usable range in the segment; else discard the segment.
+4. **SNR gate.** Require the late-reverb-to-noise ratio >= 15 dB. ACE's own noise sweep had
+   conditions at 18, 12 and −1 dB; at −1 dB every published system degrades sharply.
+5. **Minimum number of segments.** >= 8–20 independent decays per band per aggregation window
+   before that band is "decided".
+6. **Aggregate by median, not mean.** Per-segment T60 estimates have a heavy right tail (the
+   divergence above); the median over segments is the estimator, the mean is not.
+7. **Consistency check.** R² >= 0.98 in the log domain over the fit range, and require
+   |T30 − T20| / T20 <= 0.10. If T30 >> T20, the fit hit the floor — discard.
+8. **Band-limit.** Decide only in 250 Hz–4 kHz octave bands (or 1/3-octave 500 Hz–4 kHz) that
+   carry >= 10 dB more energy than the inter-band floor. Below 250 Hz you measure HVAC rumble and
+   phone handling; above 4 kHz you measure air absorption, the mic response and the codec.
+9. **Clamp and refuse.** Clamp to [0.05, 2.5] s and emit "no estimate" when any guard fails.
+   Missing is a first-class input to the classifier, not a value to impute.
+
+## 4. Which features actually separate the five classes
+
+Measured anchors from the repo's own analysis of the MIT IR Survey (Traer & McDermott 2016),
+median T20-equivalent RT60 at 250 Hz / 1 kHz / 4 kHz, in docs/research/virtual-room-presets.md section 5.1:
+
+| Class | RT60 250 / 1k / 4k (s) | Note |
+|---|---|---|
+| Bedroom | 0.29 / 0.30 / 0.27 | |
+| Living room | 0.33 / 0.32 / 0.27 | |
+| Classroom | 0.52 / 0.37 / 0.23 | |
+| Train carriage | 0.43 / 0.25 / 0.31 | |
+| Subway station | 1.34 / 0.72 / 0.49 | long space |
+| Outside | 0.06 / 0.04 / 0.02 | DRR ≈ +13 dB; essentially no tail |
+
+**Consequence:** RT60 separates *outside* (trivially) and *station*; bedroom / living room /
+classroom / train sit within 0.25–0.5 s of each other, i.e. **inside the error bar of the best
+blind estimators**. This is also why the repo already decided bedroom and living room are one
+class ("home", section 5.1 lines 419-420). The deliverable is effectively **4 classes: home,
+classroom, train, outside/street**.
+
+Feature ranking for those classes:
+1. **Background noise: level, band shape and temporal structure.** Train = broadband LF rumble
+   plus periodic 1–3 Hz vibration; classroom = speech babble; living room = TV/music; outside =
+   wind/traffic/birds with no tail. This is a scene-classification problem and the strongest
+   single signal — YAMNet-class TFLite on the phone mic, already the Stage 6 plan.
+2. **RT60 per band, when it is available at all** — it is the only feature that identifies
+   *outside* and the only one that scales the room *size*.
+3. **DRR.** The most discriminative *acoustic* parameter (outside +13 dB, train ≈ −6 dB), but
+   blind DRR error is ~2.9 dB (CRNN above) and absolute DRR needs a source-distance assumption
+   that ambient audio does not give you. Use as a weak feature.
+4. **RT_4k / RT_1k ratio** (air + surface absorption): MIT medians give 0.84 living room, 0.62
+   classroom — plausible but high-variance.
+5. **Modulation depth** (2–8 Hz MTF): the fallback when there are no clean decays.
+6. **C50 / EDT:** ~5.9 dB MAE blind and strongly correlated with DRR and RT60; adds little.
+7. **Inter-channel coherence on the phone's 2-mic array:** ~15 cm spacing is unambiguous only
+   below ≈ c/2d ≈ 1.1 kHz, and hand/body occlusion is uncontrolled. Second-order feature.
+8. Earbud IMU head orientation: irrelevant to room identity; phone accelerometer and barometer
+   are the useful non-acoustic inputs (already Stage 6).
+
+## 5. Minimum audio duration
+
+- One decay: you must observe >= 20–30 dB of decay; for RT60 = 0.5 s that is 0.17–0.25 s, plus
+  the segment context.
+- Published inputs: ML estimates from 1 s frames (Löllmann & Vary); Gamper's CNN produces one
+  estimate from a **4 s** sample; the universal CRNN uses **8 s** chunks; the ACE analysis
+  compares utterances **<15 s vs >=15 s** and finds lower error for the longer ones.
+- Practical target: accumulate a **20–30 s window containing >= 10 decays per band**, which in a
+  room with a TV or conversation converges in **30–60 s**; a ±0.1 s stable estimate needs roughly
+  **1–3 minutes** of ordinary activity. Because RT60 is a stationary property, collect it
+  opportunistically in the first few minutes of a session and refresh every few minutes rather
+  than running continuously.
+- Genuinely silent room: **no duration helps.** Return "unknown".
+
+## 6. On-device cost (Pixel 9 Pro XL class)
+
+| Component | Compute | Memory |
+|---|---|---|
+| 6-band octave filterbank + 100 Hz envelope + streaming EDC/segment detection | ~3–10 MFLOP/s, well under 1 % of one big core | < 1 MB (band envelopes + rolling energy window) |
+| ML T60, small CNN/CRNN | 369 K params (CRNN, arXiv:2109.14436) to 1.66 M (CNN baseline); ~10–100 MFLOPs per 4–8 s window → a few ms to ~30 ms per inference int8 | 1.5–7 MB fp32 per 0.4–1.7 M params, /4 with int8 |
+| Attention model (SS-BRPE class) | larger; only worth it if measured | 10–25 MB+ typical |
+| YAMNet-class scene tagger (Stage 6 plan) | existing plan, 16 kHz mono, duty-cycled | existing plan |
+
+These are estimates from published parameter counts and generic mobile inference, not device
+measurements. For scale, this repo already measured the whole app process at 0.805 of a core
+and the render at ~0.10 core on the Pixel 7a, and set a "CPU is measured on device" rule — so
+the DSP path is comfortably free and the net must be duty-cycled.
+
+## 7. One extra option: the excitation that already exists
+
+The app knows exactly what it is playing. The phone's UNPROCESSED mics hear the music leaking
+from the in-ear buds; cross-correlating the app's own reference signal with that capture
+recovers a **true room IR with no sound added to the room**, which yields absolute DRR, C50 and
+RT60 rather than RT60 alone. Caveats: in-ear Buds leak very little (expect roughly −30 to
+−50 dB; must be measured on the device), the phone may be in a pocket, and it works only while
+music plays — which is exactly the case where ambient excitation is weakest. Worth one
+measurement spike; not a base design.
+
+## 8. Build order
+
+1. Phone array, UNPROCESSED, mono (or the less-occluded channel), 250 Hz–4 kHz octave bands;
+   EDC + guarded free-decay fitting (section 3) with per-band "no estimate".
+2. Modulation/envelope features (2–8 Hz) as the steady-noise fallback.
+3. A small classifier (logistic regression / GBM) over: RT60 per band *if available*,
+   RT_4k/RT_1k, a DRR proxy, noise band levels + their temporal variance, modulation depth,
+   accelerometer/barometer. Plus YAMNet for the semantic noise class. Four classes: home,
+   classroom, train, outside.
+4. Only then a TFLite T60 net as an auxiliary feature — its real-recording error (~0.17–0.25 s)
+   is the same order as the DSP estimator's.
+5. Earbuds: not for decays. At most the already-known MAESTRO Dosimeter.SubscribeToLiveDb
+   level scalar as a non-decay level feature.
+
